#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:68
msgid "A new draft has the number ``XXXXX``. Promotion gives it the next number and opens it for discussion. A state change needs ``--reason``, and ``committed`` also needs ``--evidence``. ``--dry-run`` shows a change without making it. These are the defaults. The next section changes them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:73
msgid "A record starts with its metadata, one field per line:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:87
msgid "Authors and labels are JSON lists. ``last-updated`` may stand for ``updated``. A record may also carry ``category``, ``status``, and ``summary``. ``dsc`` keeps ``status`` in step with the state. HTML and PDF show the fields as Number, Title, Authors, Updated, Labels, and so on, with the lists as plain text."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:92
msgid "Configure the lifecycle and numbering"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:95
msgid "``discussion.toml`` holds the catalog's settings. ``init`` writes every one of them, with comments. ``dsc config`` prints the settings in effect. A catalog without the file follows the defaults below. They are the process of Guidedog's own GDS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:132
msgid "``[workflow.states.NAME]`` defines a state. ``next`` lists the states the ``state`` command may move a record to. ``evidence = true`` asks for ``--evidence`` on a move into the state. ``status`` is the text a record's optional ``status`` field takes there. A state with no ``next`` is closed. Promotion is the only way from the draft state to the open state, because promotion gives the number. Without a number, a draft may move only to the states its own ``next`` lists, such as ``abandoned``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:139
msgid "Numbers have at least ``width`` digits, padded with zeros. A number that needs more digits gets them, so 10000 follows 9999, and lists sort by value. Promotion numbers from ``start``. ``limit``, when set, is the highest number it gives. Changing ``start`` or ``limit`` later is safe. Changing ``width`` is not, once records are numbered: their numbers would no longer match it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:145
msgid "``dsc`` checks the file whenever it reads the catalog. A mistake names the line and what to write instead:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:160
msgid "The process draft that ``init`` writes describes the lifecycle we configured. It has a table of the states and a diagram of the moves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:163
msgid "Build"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:171
msgid "``dsc build`` writes the website to ``_build/html`` and one PDF per record to ``_build/pdf``. Naming a record writes only its PDF, to ``_build/selected``, and leaves the complete output alone. Warnings stop the build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:175
msgid "``dsc`` runs the ``guidedog`` named by ``--guidedog=PATH``, then by ``DSC_GUIDEDOG``, then the one on ``PATH``. ``dscc`` builds with the Guidedog inside it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:178
msgid "When an update is interrupted"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:181
msgid "Commands that change records hold the catalog's lock, ``.eds.lock``. An update of several files first writes a journal, ``.eds-journal.json``. If it stops part way, the next command says so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:190
msgid "Recovery puts back the files as they were. It refuses to overwrite a file someone edited since. ``dsc`` also checks each change before it writes anything. It refuses an empty or cut-short record, and an index without its generated region."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/discussions.rst:194
msgid "``dsc`` keeps only each record's metadata in memory. It reads a record whole only to change it. So checking a large catalog stays fast and small."
msgstr ""

