#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:1
msgid "Document a codebase"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:4
msgid "A public name needs a contract. We explain its input, its result, its ownership, and the conditions under which it fails. A signature alone is not a manual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:7
msgid "Static extraction architecture"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:10
msgid "Guidedog extracts API documentation by parsing source files into abstract syntax trees. It never runs or imports our code. So there are no side effects, no runtime to install, and no foreign dependencies to resolve:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:16
msgid "Code source trees are parsed into ASTs statically, transformed into virtual API documents, and integrated into cross-references and objects.inv."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:16
msgid "Guidedog static code documentation architecture."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:65
msgid "Rust"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:74
msgid "Guidedog reads each crate's source with tree-sitter and follows its module tree the way rustc does: ``Cargo.toml``'s ``[lib] path``, then ``src/lib.rs`` or ``src/main.rs``. It does not compile, run, or expand the documented crate. Items made by macros or build scripts are therefore absent. A ``rust_autoapi_crates`` entry may also be a single root ``.rs`` file or a folder of loose ``.rs`` files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:80
msgid "Rustdoc's rules decide what appears. A ``pub`` item reachable through ``pub`` modules is documented in its module. ``pub use`` of an item from a private module documents it under the re-exported name. ``#[doc(hidden)]`` items, ``#[cfg(test)]`` modules and tests are left out. ``rust_autoapi_options`` adds ``private-members`` (everything, with its visibility shown), ``hidden-members``, or ``noindex``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:86
msgid "Doc comments are CommonMark. Guidedog translates them into the page. ``# Examples`` and the other headings become rubrics. Fenced code follows rustdoc's language rules. Intra-doc links (``[`Parser`]``, ``[Parser::next]``) become ``rust`` domain references. A link into the crate that names nothing is a ``rust.autodoc.link`` warning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:91
msgid "The pages are written under ``rust_autoapi_root``. There is an index page, then one page per documented module (``my_crate.rst``, ``my_crate/parser.rst``). With ``rust_autoapi_add_toctree_entry`` (the default), the index joins the root document's toctree. With ``rust_autoapi_generate_api_docs = false``, Guidedog reads the settings without generating pages. :doc:`../topics/domains` describes the ``rust`` directives and roles. Hand-written pages can use them too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:98
msgid "Odin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:107
msgid "Guidedog reads Odin source by static analysis, across every build target at once. It does not compile or run the documented package. The generated pages join the document graph and object inventory. See :doc:`../odin` for directives, signatures, and options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:112
msgid "Python"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:120
msgid "Guidedog parses Python source rather than importing it. This avoids import side effects. It also means runtime-generated objects may be absent. When such objects are part of the public interface, we describe them explicitly. See :doc:`../autodoc` for the exact discovery rules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:125
msgid "Link across projects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/document-code.rst:128
msgid "We publish the generated ``objects.inv`` with the site. An intersphinx mapping in another project can then resolve our names. Explicit mappings keep builds reproducible. See :doc:`../intersphinx`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:1
msgid "Publish to GitHub Pages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:4
msgid "We can publish a Guidedog site to GitHub Pages in one of two ways. GitHub Actions can build and deploy it on each push, with released Guidedog and GitHub's official Pages actions, and no personal token. Or ``guidedog-pages`` can publish it from our own machine, whenever we ask or whenever the sources change. We use one route per site, because a repository has one Pages source: GitHub Actions or a branch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:12
msgid "Route 1 uses GitHub Actions CI/CD; Route 2 uses the local guidedog-pages publisher with a durable journal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:12
msgid "The two routes for publishing documentation to GitHub Pages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:44
msgid "Publish from this machine in one command"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:47
msgid "From the repository that holds our documentation, we run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:53
msgid "On a machine that has never published, it does each missing step once and says what it does. It asks before anything that changes GitHub or this machine's permissions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:56
msgid "**Configuration.** Without ``pages.toml`` it reads the repository from ``git remote get-url origin`` (HTTPS or SSH GitHub URLs) and asks us to confirm it. It finds the nearest project (a folder with ``conf.toml``, here, in ``docs``, or above, up to the repository's root), and writes ``pages.toml``. We can name both ourselves with ``--repository=OWNER/REPO`` and ``--project=DIR``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:61
msgid "**Token.** Without a stored token it offers to sign in with a code on github.com (GitHub's device flow). The token goes to the macOS Keychain, the Windows Credential Manager, or the Secret Service of a Linux desktop. When the GitHub CLI is signed in, it offers instead to use ``gh auth token`` *for this run only*. The GitHub CLI's token is never stored: it belongs to ``gh``, which refreshes and revokes it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:66
msgid "**Authorization.** It shows the account, the repository with its GitHub ID, the branch, and the Pages settings, and asks once whether this machine may publish there. A branch that holds work Guidedog did not publish is never replaced without our explicit answer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:70
msgid "**Pages source.** If Pages deploys from Actions or another branch, it asks before pointing Pages at the branch. A repository without a Pages site is set up after the first push, which makes the branch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:73
msgid "**Publication.** It builds the project and pushes the whole site in one commit. It waits for GitHub Pages to deploy that commit and prints the live URL."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:76
msgid "The next time, every step is done already. ``publish`` then builds and publishes without a question."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:79
msgid "In a script, or with ``--yes``, it never asks. ``--yes`` answers the confirmations (repository, authorization, Pages source) with yes. It still never signs in and never replaces others' work. A step that needs us fails with ``pages.guide`` and a hint that names the flag or the step command, such as ``--repository=octo/manual`` or ``guidedog-pages auth login``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:85
msgid "Publish from this machine, step by step"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:88
msgid "The step commands stay for scripts and for checking each part. ``guidedog-pages`` builds the project with the installed ``guidedog``. It pushes the whole site to a branch of its own in one Git commit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:92
msgid "We write ``pages.toml`` beside our project. It names the repository, the branch, and the credential profile. It holds no secret, so we can commit it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:99
msgid "Next we store a token for the profile. We make a fine-grained personal access token for the repository with **Contents: read and write**, and **Pages: read** so that publication can confirm the deployment. If we want ``setup`` to configure Pages, it also needs **Pages: write** and **Administration: write**. We paste it when asked. It is not shown, and it never goes to a file:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:110
msgid "``auth login`` signs in with a code instead, for a GitHub App or OAuth App we registered with the device flow enabled: ``guidedog-pages auth login --client-id=YOUR_APP_ID``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:114
msgid "We authorize the target, point Pages at the branch, and check that everything is ready:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:122
msgid "``bind`` refuses a branch that holds work Guidedog did not publish. ``--adopt`` lets the next publication replace it. The branch must not be the repository's default branch. Publication goes only to a bound target. Guidedog refuses, until we bind again, a ``pages.toml`` that names another repository, a repository that was renamed, transferred, or made again, and another account's token."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:133
msgid "``publish`` builds with warnings as errors (``strict = true``) and ``html_baseurl`` set to ``site_url``. It lists what it adds, changes, and removes. Then it sends one push that holds only the files the branch lacks. GitHub moves the branch only if it still has the commit that was read, so a publication never overwrites a newer one. A ``.nojekyll`` file is added, so that GitHub serves folders such as ``_static``. Then it waits, up to ``wait_seconds``, for GitHub Pages to deploy that commit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:140
msgid "The exit status says what happened:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:142
msgid "0: the site is live or was already published."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:143
msgid "1: nothing was published."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:144
msgid "2: a usage, configuration, or missing step problem."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:145
msgid "6: GitHub accepted the publication but has not confirmed the deployment yet."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:146
msgid "7: it is not known whether the branch moved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:148
msgid "We check later with ``guidedog-pages status``. ``guidedog-pages recover`` settles a publication whose answer was lost, and it never moves the branch back."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:152
msgid "To publish a site we build ourselves, we write ``site = \"build/site\"`` instead of ``project``. The folder is copied and checked before it is published."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:155
msgid "Publish on every change, with a preview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:158
msgid "``guidedog-pages watch`` publishes once, then again whenever the sources change and have been quiet for ``debounce_ms`` (at most ``max_wait_ms`` after the first change). A failed build or publication is reported, and the published site stays as it was."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:162
msgid "``guidedog-pages watch --serve`` also serves the built site on this machine, at ``http://127.0.0.1:8000/`` (``--serve=PORT`` for another port). We see each build before GitHub Pages deploys it. One Ctrl+C stops both the watch and the preview."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:166
msgid "A scheduler can keep it running. On macOS, a launch agent in ``~/Library/LaunchAgents/org.example.docs.plist``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:187
msgid "On Linux, a systemd user service, ``~/.config/systemd/user/docs-pages.service``, started with ``systemctl --user enable --now docs-pages``. A user service reaches the desktop's Secret Service. On a server without one, we give the token with ``--credential=env`` and an ``Environment=GUIDEDOG_PAGES_TOKEN=...`` drop-in readable only by our own account:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:206
msgid "On Windows, a scheduled task at logon, so that the Credential Manager is available:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:215
msgid "Build and deploy with GitHub Actions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:218
msgid "We generate the workflow instead of copying it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:225
msgid "It writes ``.github/workflows/docs-pages.yml`` for a project in ``docs`` and a source branch ``main`` (``--project=DIR``, ``--branch=NAME``). The workflow installs this Guidedog release (``--version=X``). The archive's SHA-256 is read from the release's ``SHA256SUMS``, or given with ``--sha256``. ``--offline`` writes a clearly marked placeholder. The workflow's checksum test fails on it, and the command exits with status 1 until we give ``--sha256``. Without ``--write`` it prints the workflow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:232
msgid "In the repository's **Settings -> Pages**, we choose **GitHub Actions** as the publishing source and restrict the ``github-pages`` environment to ``main``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:235
msgid "The workflow it writes is this one (for Guidedog 0.6.3):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:320
msgid "The build job can read sources. Only the deploy job receives Pages and OIDC permissions. GitHub supplies its short-lived credentials, so we add no personal token. The checkout keeps no credentials. Pull requests build but never deploy. The workflow stays on ``pull_request``, never ``pull_request_target``. Action commits are pinned by SHA with their versions in comments. The Guidedog archive is checked against its SHA-256 before it is unpacked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:327
msgid "Check an existing workflow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:334
msgid "It reports an older Guidedog release (``pages.workflow-outdated``), a missing or placeholder checksum, an action pinned to a tag or to another commit than the reviewed one (``pages.workflow-unpinned``, ``pages.workflow-pin``), ``pull_request_target``, and a checkout that keeps its credentials, each with its line and the fix. We update with ``guidedog-pages workflow --write --force``. Action updates are a separate review: the pinned commits are listed in ``gd-pages`` and change only with a reviewed release."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:341
msgid "If the project selects build plugins, we install and register their trusted executables in the build job before ``guidedog build``. Registrations on our own computer are not copied to a runner. See :doc:`../plugins`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:347
msgid "A site built from a private repository is not necessarily private. We check its Pages visibility before publishing confidential material."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:350
msgid "Tokens and automation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:353
msgid "``--credential=env`` takes the token from ``GUIDEDOG_PAGES_TOKEN`` for one command and never stores it. ``GUIDEDOG_PAGES_CREDENTIAL=env`` chooses the same for runs that cannot take the option, such as ``guidedog publish``. A missing or locked credential store is reported. The token is never written to a plain file instead. ``auth logout`` deletes the stored token. We revoke it at GitHub too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:359
msgid "Through guidedog publish"
msgstr ""

#: ../../howto/github-pages.rst:362
msgid "``guidedog publish pages`` runs the same guided publish with Guidedog's own engine. ``guidedog-pages`` is also a publisher program for ``guidedog publish --publisher=pages`` (see :doc:`../plugins`)."
msgstr ""

